---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const systems = [
  { key: '', label: 'All' },
  { key: 'steel', label: 'Steel' },
  { key: 'concrete', label: 'Concrete' },
  { key: 'panel', label: 'MSE panels' },
  { key: 'ballast', label: 'Ballast' },
  { key: 'rail', label: 'Rail' },
  { key: 'tie', label: 'Ties' },
];

const progression = [
  {
    src: '/lab/trra/prog-clay.webp',
    alt: 'The same wide frame with untextured grey and green geometry.',
    step: '01',
    note: 'Geometry only. Ground cover, roads and buildings pulled from the map, still flat colour.',
  },
  {
    src: '/lab/trra/prog-ortho.webp',
    alt: 'The same frame with aerial photography draped over a single continuous terrain.',
    step: '02',
    note: 'One continuous terrain, public domain USGS aerial draped on it by world position.',
  },
  {
    src: '/lab/trra/prog-final.webp',
    alt: 'The same frame with vegetation, warm morning light and finished materials.',
    step: '03',
    note: 'Real vegetation, building facades, mid morning sun at 40 degrees.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="The MacArthur · Lab · Brett Boggs"
  description="A rail bridge approach in St. Louis, rebuilt to scale from public data so a job could be shown instead of described."
  ogImage="/lab/trra/hero-1200.webp"
>
  <article>
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Lab · Aug 2026</p>
      <h1>The MacArthur</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A retaining wall and bridge approach on the Terminal Railroad's MacArthur crossing, built to
        scale inside a copy of the site.
      </p>
    </header>

    <figure class="shot">
      <img
        src="/lab/trra/hero.webp"
        alt="Aerial view of the MacArthur Bridge crossing the Mississippi into downtown St. Louis, with the retaining wall and rail approach in the foreground."
        width="2000"
        height="1125"
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
      <figcaption>Looking north west across the Mississippi. Downtown and the Arch sit where they sit.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        My brother had a job to price. Plans describe what gets built. They are not much use for
        showing someone what it is. So the site got rebuilt: the river, the floodplain, the bridge,
        the yard, downtown, all of it to scale, with the job standing in the middle of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing here is invented. Alignments, roads, buildings and tree cover come from OpenStreetMap,
        the ground photography from the USGS National Map, which is public domain. Where the drawings
        and the map disagreed, the drawings won and the difference got written down.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="viewer" aria-label="Interactive model">
      <div class="viewer-head">
        <h2>The model</h2>
        <p class="viewer-hint"><span class="hint-pointer">Drag to orbit. Right drag to pan. Scroll to zoom.</span><span class="hint-touch">One finger orbits. Two pan and pinch.</span></p>
      </div>

      <div class="stage">
        <canvas id="trra-canvas"></canvas>
        <p class="stage-note" id="trra-status">Loading</p>
      </div>

      <div class="controls">
        <div class="control-group" role="group" aria-label="Extent">
          <span class="control-label">Extent</span>
          <div class="control-row" id="trra-extent">
            <button type="button" data-url="/lab/trra/job.glb" class="is-on">Job scope</button>
            <button type="button" data-url="/lab/trra/network.glb">Full network</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="control-group" role="group" aria-label="Ground">
          <span class="control-label">Ground</span>
          <div class="control-row" id="trra-ground">
            <button type="button" data-ground="off" class="is-on">Off</button>
            <button type="button" data-ground="on">World</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="control-group">
          <span class="control-label">View</span>
          <div class="control-row">
            <button type="button" id="trra-recenter">Recenter</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="control-group" role="group" aria-label="System">
          <span class="control-label">System</span>
          <div class="control-row" id="trra-systems">
            {
              systems.map((s) => (
                <button type="button" data-system={s.key} class={s.key === '' ? 'is-on' : ''}>
                  {s.label}
                </button>
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="viewer-caption">
        Six meshes, one per material system, exported from the same file the renders come out of.
        Isolating a system is the question anyone actually asks in front of a render. Turning the
        ground on brings in the rest of the scene: the terrain, downtown and the built context as
        real geometry, with the ground itself carrying a Cycles render of the world shot straight
        down. That is another megabyte, so it is only fetched if you ask for it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Where this is</h2>
      <p>
        The scene sits on a validated transform between the model and the real world. Feeding the
        anchors through it lands the Gateway Arch within 19 m of its published position and Busch
        Stadium within 8 m, which is inside the accuracy of the aerial itself. So the job can be
        drawn straight onto the photograph rather than pointed at.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="locator">
      <div class="locator-frame">
        <img
          src="/lab/trra/locator.webp"
          alt="Aerial photograph of the Mississippi at St. Louis with the MacArthur crossing marked, the Gateway Arch and Busch Stadium labelled for orientation."
          width="1400"
          height="1400"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <svg viewBox="0 0 1000 1000" aria-hidden="true">
          <line class="reach" x1="592.0" y1="574.5" x2="706.0" y2="581.0" />
          <line class="job" x1="388.2" y1="504.4" x2="592.0" y2="574.5" />
          <circle class="pin" cx="592.0" cy="574.5" r="9" />
          <circle class="dot" cx="459.5" cy="171.4" r="5" />
          <text class="tag" x="474" y="176">Gateway Arch</text>
          <circle class="dot" cx="228.3" cy="248.0" r="5" />
          <text class="tag" x="243" y="253">Busch Stadium</text>
          <line class="rule" x1="60" y1="946" x2="228.4" y2="946" />
          <text class="tag" x="60" y="932">500 m</text>
          <line class="rule" x1="946" y1="946" x2="946" y2="898" />
          <text class="tag" x="939" y="884">N</text>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Bridge and wall in sienna, the rebuilt Illinois approach in ochre. Base imagery USGS National
        Map, public domain, 1.45 m per pixel.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The wall</h2>
      <p>
        3,380 precast panels at five feet square, 19 mm joints, set 55 mm proud in running bond.
        Courses are level on one datum across every face. Grade trims the bottom of the stack and the
        coping closes the top, which is how the real thing goes up and the only way the joint lines
        stay straight in a render.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="shot">
      <img
        src="/lab/trra/wall.webp"
        alt="Close view of the mechanically stabilised earth wall with the truss bridge behind it."
        width="1800"
        height="1013"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>The wall, from the angle it will be looked at.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Track that would actually run</h2>
      <p>
        Every approach is one continuous swept mesh along a faired alignment, so stepping is
        impossible by construction. The joints were checked with numbers rather than eyes. Worst gap
        across the whole build is 5.27 mm, and the railhead never steps more than a tenth of a
        millimetre. Grades stay under two percent and the tightest curve is 151 m.
      </p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Run</th><th>Length</th><th>Tightest R</th><th>Max grade</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Wye, Illinois</td><td>1,250 m</td><td>280 m</td><td>1.90%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Main east</td><td>1,274 m</td><td>straight</td><td>0.97%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>West approach</td><td>997 m</td><td>151 m</td><td>1.50%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Missouri branch</td><td>800 m</td><td>502 m</td><td>1.80%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Illinois highway</td><td>1,633 m</td><td>297 m</td><td>0.66%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Three passes at the same frame</h2>
      <p>
        Ground was the whole problem. Procedural tiles cannot hold up at 800 m because a three metre
        tile is a few pixels. Real aerial photography on real terrain is what makes it read.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ol class="progression">
      {
        progression.map((p) => (
          <li>
            <img src={p.src} alt={p.alt} width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
            <div class="progression-note">
              <span class="progression-step">{p.step}</span>
              <span>{p.note}</span>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>

    <figure class="shot">
      <img
        src="/lab/trra/yard.webp"
        alt="Low aerial over the floodplain showing the trestle, trees and aerial photography on the ground."
        width="1400"
        height="788"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>Floodplain and trestle. Trees sit only inside real wood polygons, cleared off every road and track.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What is in the scene</h2>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Trees and scrub</td><td>12,185, scattered inside 194 ha of mapped wood</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Buildings</td><td>2,629 footprints at surveyed height, tallest 192 m</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Roads</td><td>1,609 polylines by class, plus 44 floodplain tracks</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Terrain</td><td>One heightfield, 12 m grid, 131k vertices</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Aerial</td><td>0.61 m per pixel over 5 km, 0.31 m over the job</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Arch</td><td>The published weighted catenary. 630.0 ft, checked</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Render</td><td>Cycles on GPU, 400 adaptive samples, 2560 by 1440</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Still open</h2>
      <p>
        The wye bents are concrete columns where the original are braced lattice towers, so the styles
        clash at that joint. Guardrails match the envelope of the original, not its post and rail
        detail. The west approach is a clean arc that departs on the right tangent and lands on the
        right point, but wanders up to 40 m off the traced line in between. None of it is job scope.
        All of it is written down.
      </p>
    </div>

    <a class="back-link" href="/lab/">Back to Lab</a>
  </article>
</Base>

<script>
  import { mountTrraViewer } from '../../scripts/trraViewer';

  const canvas = document.getElementById('trra-canvas') as HTMLCanvasElement | null;
  const status = document.getElementById('trra-status');
  if (canvas) {
    const viewer = mountTrraViewer(canvas, '/lab/trra/job.glb', (text) => {
      if (status) status.textContent = text;
    });

    if (!viewer) {
      // no WebGL: the controls would be inert, so retire them and leave the
      // stills and the text, which are the majority of the page anyway
      document.querySelector('.controls')?.setAttribute('hidden', '');
      document.querySelector('.viewer-hint')?.setAttribute('hidden', '');
    }

    if (viewer) {
      const arm = (id: string, run: (btn: HTMLButtonElement) => void) => {
        const host = document.getElementById(id);
        host?.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
          const btn = (event.target as HTMLElement).closest('button');
          if (!btn || !host.contains(btn)) return;
          host.querySelectorAll('button').forEach((b) => b.classList.toggle('is-on', b === btn));
          run(btn as HTMLButtonElement);
        });
      };

      arm('trra-systems', (btn) => viewer.isolate(btn.dataset.system ?? ''));
      arm('trra-ground', (btn) => viewer.setGround(btn.dataset.ground === 'on'));
      document.getElementById('trra-recenter')?.addEventListener('click', () => viewer.recenter());
      arm('trra-extent', (btn) => {
        const url = btn.dataset.url;
        if (url) viewer.setModel(url);
      });
    }
  }
</script>

<style>
  .shot {
    margin: 3rem 0 0;
  }
  .shot img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 2px solid var(--ink);
  }
  .shot figcaption,
  .stage-note,
  .control-label,
  .progression-note {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .shot figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
  }

  .locator {
    margin: 3rem 0 0;
  }
  .locator-frame {
    position: relative;
    border: 2px solid var(--ink);
    line-height: 0;
  }
  .locator-frame img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
  .locator-frame svg {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
  }
  .locator .job {
    stroke: var(--sienna);
    stroke-width: 7;
    stroke-linecap: round;
  }
  .locator .reach {
    stroke: var(--sunflower);
    stroke-width: 4.5;
    stroke-linecap: round;
  }
  .locator .pin {
    fill: none;
    stroke: #fff;
    stroke-width: 3;
  }
  .locator .dot {
    fill: none;
    stroke: #fff;
    stroke-width: 2.5;
  }
  .locator .rule {
    stroke: #fff;
    stroke-width: 4;
  }
  .locator .tag {
    fill: #fff;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 15px;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    paint-order: stroke;
    stroke: rgba(24, 20, 14, 0.55);
    stroke-width: 3.5;
  }
  .locator figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .viewer {
    margin-top: 4rem;
  }
  .viewer-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ink);
    padding-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  .viewer-head h2 {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .hint-touch {
    display: none;
  }
  @media (hover: none) and (pointer: coarse) {
    .hint-pointer {
      display: none;
    }
    .hint-touch {
      display: inline;
    }
  }
  .viewer-hint {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.76rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .stage {
    position: relative;
    margin-top: 1.2rem;
    border: 2px solid var(--ink);
    background: var(--paper);
  }
  .stage canvas {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: clamp(300px, 56vw, 620px);
    /* one finger keeps scrolling the page; two fingers drive the model */
    touch-action: pan-y;
  }
  .stage-note {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0.6rem;
    bottom: 0.5rem;
    margin: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* it sits over aerial photography once the ground is on, so it carries its
       own backing rather than relying on the paper behind it */
    padding: 0.2rem 0.45rem;
    background: rgba(244, 237, 223, 0.82);
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* touch only: the viewer stays inert until tapped so the page can still be
     scrolled past it, then hands back one finger orbit and two finger pan */
  .stage :global(.stage-grab),
  .stage :global(.stage-release) {
    position: absolute;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    border: 2px solid var(--ink);
    border-radius: 0;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .stage :global(.stage-grab) {
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    border: 0;
    background: rgba(244, 237, 223, 0.34);
    color: var(--ink);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .stage :global(.stage-release) {
    top: 0.6rem;
    right: 0.6rem;
    padding: 0.3rem 0.7rem;
    background: var(--ink);
    color: var(--paper);
  }
  .stage :global([hidden]) {
    display: none;
  }

  .controls {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 1.6rem 2.4rem;
    margin-top: 1.1rem;
  }
  .control-group {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }
  .control-label {
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
  }
  .control-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.45rem;
  }
  .control-row button {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--ink);
    background: transparent;
    border: 2px solid rgba(51, 41, 28, 0.28);
    border-radius: 0;
    padding: 0.35rem 0.75rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition:
      border-color 0.25s var(--ease-warm),
      background 0.25s var(--ease-warm);
  }
  .control-row button:hover {
    border-color: var(--ink);
  }
  .control-row button.is-on {
    border-color: var(--ink);
    background: var(--ink);
    color: var(--paper);
  }

  .viewer-caption {
    margin-top: 1.4rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .progression {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 2.4rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 2rem;
  }
  .progression img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 2px solid var(--ink);
  }
  .progression-note {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.9rem;
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
    align-items: baseline;
  }
  .progression-step {
    color: var(--sienna);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .progression {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
      gap: 1.2rem;
    }
    .progression-note {
      display: block;
    }
    .progression-step {
      display: block;
      margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
    }
  }
</style>
